1
00:00:00,079 --> 00:00:03,138
Every layer of a neural network starts with the same step.

2
00:00:03,563 --> 00:00:11,077
It takes a vector in, and it gives a new vector out, in a very special way: it respects adding and scaling.

3
00:00:11,542 --> 00:00:14,321
Functions like this are called linear mappings.

4
00:00:14,747 --> 00:00:20,398
If you understand them, you know exactly what a layer can do, and also what it can never do on its own.

5
00:00:21,339 --> 00:00:32,430
In this video you will learn the definition of a linear mapping, see what it does to a grid, test functions that are not linear, and find the matrix of any linear map from just two arrows.

6
00:00:32,868 --> 00:00:38,706
You need video one on vectors, video two on matrices, and video three on bases.

7
00:00:40,439 --> 00:00:42,899
A function takes an input and gives an output.

8
00:00:43,351 --> 00:00:47,008
Here the input is a vector, and the output is a vector too.

9
00:00:47,447 --> 00:00:48,910
We write f of x.

10
00:00:49,349 --> 00:00:55,293
The function f is linear if it respects the two moves from video one: adding and scaling.

11
00:00:56,232 --> 00:00:57,709
Here is the definition.

12
00:00:58,161 --> 00:01:07,589
For all vectors u and v, and all numbers a and b, f of a u plus b v equals a times f of u plus b times f of v.

13
00:01:07,988 --> 00:01:14,185
In words: combine first and then apply f, or apply f first and then combine.

14
00:01:14,623 --> 00:01:15,873
You must get the same result.

15
00:01:16,793 --> 00:01:18,176
Read it symbol by symbol.

16
00:01:18,614 --> 00:01:21,168
The letters u and v are input vectors.

17
00:01:21,593 --> 00:01:24,878
The letters a and b are scalars, the weights.

18
00:01:25,277 --> 00:01:28,894
On the left, we apply f to the linear combination.

19
00:01:29,332 --> 00:01:35,370
On the right, we combine the outputs, f of u and f of v, with the same weights.

20
00:01:36,906 --> 00:01:39,419
It is the same as two simpler rules.

21
00:01:39,924 --> 00:01:44,858
First, f of u plus v equals f of u plus f of v.

22
00:01:45,297 --> 00:01:49,645
Second, f of c times u equals c times f of u.

23
00:01:50,097 --> 00:01:52,252
Here is a tiny example with numbers.

24
00:01:52,677 --> 00:01:54,898
Let f of x be three x.

25
00:01:55,323 --> 00:02:03,861
Then f of two plus five is twenty-one, and f of two plus f of five is six plus fifteen, also twenty-one.

26
00:02:04,806 --> 00:02:07,080
One consequence is very useful.

27
00:02:07,519 --> 00:02:10,577
A linear map always sends zero to zero.

28
00:02:11,003 --> 00:02:11,335
Why?

29
00:02:11,761 --> 00:02:12,931
Take the scalar zero.

30
00:02:13,356 --> 00:02:19,314
Then f of zero times u equals zero times f of u, which is the zero vector.

31
00:02:19,739 --> 00:02:23,197
So if a function moves the origin, it cannot be linear.

32
00:02:24,939 --> 00:02:26,655
What does a linear map look like?

33
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:27,692
Watch a grid.

34
00:02:28,104 --> 00:02:31,229
After a linear map, the grid lines stay straight.

35
00:02:31,668 --> 00:02:33,995
Parallel lines stay parallel.

36
00:02:34,421 --> 00:02:35,790
The spacing stays even.

37
00:02:36,229 --> 00:02:38,051
And the origin stays where it is.

38
00:02:38,476 --> 00:02:40,312
That is the picture of linearity.

39
00:02:41,899 --> 00:02:43,149
Here are four examples.

40
00:02:43,601 --> 00:02:49,758
First, a stretch, which we met in video two: everything becomes twice as wide.

41
00:02:50,184 --> 00:02:53,322
Second, a rotation by ninety degrees.

42
00:02:53,787 --> 00:02:54,652
Third, a shear.

43
00:02:55,091 --> 00:03:02,830
It slides each horizontal line sideways, more and more the higher it is, like pushing the top of a deck of cards.

44
00:03:03,826 --> 00:03:06,326
Fourth, a projection onto a line.

45
00:03:06,751 --> 00:03:10,820
It drops every point straight down onto the horizontal axis.

46
00:03:11,259 --> 00:03:13,360
The whole plane collapses onto one line.

47
00:03:13,786 --> 00:03:24,318
That may look extreme, but the rules still hold: the lines stay straight and evenly spaced, the origin does not move, and adding and scaling are respected.

48
00:03:26,026 --> 00:03:28,526
Now some functions that are not linear.

49
00:03:29,018 --> 00:03:32,302
First, f of x equals x plus one.

50
00:03:32,741 --> 00:03:35,574
It shifts every number one step to the right.

51
00:03:36,013 --> 00:03:40,281
Check the origin: f of zero is one, not zero.

52
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:43,725
The origin moved, so this function is not linear.

53
00:03:44,673 --> 00:03:45,763
Check the rule as well.

54
00:03:46,188 --> 00:03:50,550
f of two plus three is f of five, which is six.

55
00:03:50,989 --> 00:03:56,082
But f of two plus f of three is three plus four, which is seven.

56
00:03:56,508 --> 00:03:57,798
Six is not seven.

57
00:03:58,223 --> 00:04:03,396
A shift like this is called an affine map: a linear map followed by a move.

58
00:04:03,821 --> 00:04:06,574
It keeps lines straight, but it moves the origin.

59
00:04:08,119 --> 00:04:11,377
Second, f of x equals x squared.

60
00:04:11,789 --> 00:04:15,819
f of one plus one is f of two, which is four.

61
00:04:16,244 --> 00:04:20,726
But f of one plus f of one is one plus one, which is two.

62
00:04:21,178 --> 00:04:21,976
Four is not two.

63
00:04:22,401 --> 00:04:30,234
Scaling fails too: f of two times three is thirty-six, but two times f of three is only eighteen.

64
00:04:31,726 --> 00:04:32,564
Look at the graphs.

65
00:04:33,002 --> 00:04:37,896
A linear function of one number is a straight line through the origin, like two x.

66
00:04:38,335 --> 00:04:42,790
The shift, x plus one, is straight but misses the origin.

67
00:04:43,229 --> 00:04:45,596
The square, x squared, is curved.

68
00:04:46,008 --> 00:04:47,936
Only the first one is linear.

69
00:04:49,659 --> 00:04:51,787
Now the key theorem of this video.

70
00:04:52,212 --> 00:04:57,013
Every linear map between spaces like these can be written as a matrix.

71
00:04:57,438 --> 00:05:02,824
And the matrix is easy to find: its columns are where the basis vectors land.

72
00:05:03,263 --> 00:05:05,457
The first column is f of e one.

73
00:05:05,896 --> 00:05:08,329
The second column is f of e two.

74
00:05:09,273 --> 00:05:10,110
Why is this true?

75
00:05:10,536 --> 00:05:16,360
Any vector x is x one times e one plus x two times e two.

76
00:05:16,786 --> 00:05:25,363
Because f is linear, f of x equals x one times f of e one plus x two times f of e two.

77
00:05:25,788 --> 00:05:33,315
That is a linear combination of two fixed vectors, and from video two we know: that is a matrix times x.

78
00:05:34,873 --> 00:05:35,511
Let's use it.

79
00:05:35,950 --> 00:05:42,851
Suppose a linear map sends e one to two, one, and sends e two to minus one, one.

80
00:05:43,290 --> 00:05:49,141
Then its matrix A has the first column two, one, and the second column minus one, one.

81
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:51,801
Two arrows were enough to know the whole map.

82
00:05:52,706 --> 00:05:54,953
Where does the vector three, two go?

83
00:05:55,365 --> 00:06:00,871
Use the columns: three times two, one plus two times minus one, one.

84
00:06:01,283 --> 00:06:06,709
That is six minus two, which is four, and three plus two, which is five.

85
00:06:07,134 --> 00:06:09,820
So f of three, two is four, five.

86
00:06:10,259 --> 00:06:12,054
The row view gives the same answer.

87
00:06:13,573 --> 00:06:17,123
A second example: the rotation by ninety degrees.

88
00:06:17,535 --> 00:06:23,373
It sends e one to zero, one, and e two to minus one, zero.

89
00:06:23,799 --> 00:06:28,573
So its matrix has the columns zero, one and minus one, zero.

90
00:06:29,011 --> 00:06:31,977
That is exactly the rotation matrix from video two.

91
00:06:32,416 --> 00:06:33,626
Now you know where it came from.

92
00:06:35,326 --> 00:06:38,770
What if we apply two linear maps, one after the other?

93
00:06:39,209 --> 00:06:44,727
The result is linear too, and its matrix is the product of the two matrices.

94
00:06:45,220 --> 00:06:47,972
First the shear S, then the projection P.

95
00:06:48,398 --> 00:06:55,339
The combined map has the matrix P times S, with rows one, one and zero, zero.

96
00:06:56,273 --> 00:06:58,187
Check it with the vector one, two.

97
00:06:58,626 --> 00:07:00,315
The shear sends it to three, two.

98
00:07:00,741 --> 00:07:03,932
The projection then sends that to three, zero.

99
00:07:04,358 --> 00:07:09,291
And P times S applied to one, two also gives three, zero.

100
00:07:09,730 --> 00:07:11,565
One matrix does both steps.

101
00:07:13,106 --> 00:07:13,957
Two more words.

102
00:07:14,382 --> 00:07:18,917
The kernel of a linear map is the set of all inputs that it sends to zero.

103
00:07:19,356 --> 00:07:21,444
Another name is the null space.

104
00:07:21,909 --> 00:07:27,906
For the projection, every point on the vertical axis, like zero, five, drops onto the origin.

105
00:07:28,305 --> 00:07:31,484
So the kernel of the projection is the vertical axis.

106
00:07:32,439 --> 00:07:35,272
The image of a map is the set of all its outputs.

107
00:07:35,710 --> 00:07:37,479
Another name is the range.

108
00:07:37,905 --> 00:07:44,314
The projection only produces points on the horizontal axis, so its image is that axis.

109
00:07:44,753 --> 00:07:48,915
The dimension of the image is the rank of the matrix, from video three.

110
00:07:49,367 --> 00:07:50,923
Here the rank is one.

111
00:07:51,873 --> 00:07:53,229
There is a simple balance.

112
00:07:53,654 --> 00:07:59,333
The dimension of the kernel plus the dimension of the image equals the dimension of the input space.

113
00:07:59,758 --> 00:08:02,843
For the projection, one plus one equals two.

114
00:08:03,242 --> 00:08:06,873
What the map destroys, plus what it keeps, adds up to everything.

115
00:08:08,606 --> 00:08:10,029
Now, machine learning.

116
00:08:10,428 --> 00:08:16,039
A linear layer in PyTorch computes, in our notation, W times x plus b.

117
00:08:16,704 --> 00:08:23,021
W is the weight matrix, and b is the bias vector, a fixed vector that is added at the end.

118
00:08:23,446 --> 00:08:25,734
W times x is a linear map.

119
00:08:26,172 --> 00:08:31,518
Adding b moves the origin, so the whole layer is affine, not strictly linear.

120
00:08:33,099 --> 00:08:34,987
Here is the big consequence.

121
00:08:35,426 --> 00:08:38,724
Two linear maps in a row are just one linear map.

122
00:08:39,163 --> 00:08:42,408
Take two toy layers, W one and W two.

123
00:08:42,846 --> 00:08:50,479
W two times W one times x equals the single matrix W two times W one, times x.

124
00:08:50,905 --> 00:08:55,732
A stack of ten linear layers can still only do what one matrix can do.

125
00:08:57,273 --> 00:09:08,536
GPT-2, for example, has a block that expands each vector of seven hundred sixty-eight numbers to three thousand seventy-two numbers, and then brings it back.

126
00:09:09,001 --> 00:09:13,695
Without something in between, those two maps would collapse into one single map.

127
00:09:14,121 --> 00:09:18,602
That is why networks put a nonlinear activation function between the layers.

128
00:09:19,041 --> 00:09:21,701
It gets its own video, video sixteen.

129
00:09:23,206 --> 00:09:24,230
Where does all this come from?

130
00:09:24,669 --> 00:09:30,865
The definition of a linear map is exactly axiom A: it respects adding and scaling.

131
00:09:31,291 --> 00:09:36,490
Matrices, composition, kernel and image all follow from that one rule.

132
00:09:38,206 --> 00:09:38,685
Your turn.

133
00:09:39,123 --> 00:09:40,905
Pause the video and try this.

134
00:09:41,331 --> 00:09:45,041
The map f sends x, y to two x, x plus y.

135
00:09:45,453 --> 00:09:46,118
Is it linear?

136
00:09:46,530 --> 00:09:48,206
If yes, find its matrix.

137
00:09:48,645 --> 00:09:51,650
Hint: look at where e one and e two go.

138
00:09:52,573 --> 00:09:53,317
It is linear.

139
00:09:53,743 --> 00:09:57,586
f of e one is f of one, zero, which is two, one.

140
00:09:58,025 --> 00:10:02,971
f of e two is f of zero, one, which is zero, one.

141
00:10:03,410 --> 00:10:07,373
So the matrix has the columns two, one and zero, one.

142
00:10:07,799 --> 00:10:14,554
Check with three, one: six, four from the formula, and also six, four from the matrix.

143
00:10:16,106 --> 00:10:16,904
Second question.

144
00:10:17,329 --> 00:10:22,675
Is the function g of x equals five x minus three linear?

145
00:10:23,114 --> 00:10:25,361
Pause and use the fastest test you know.

146
00:10:26,273 --> 00:10:28,640
The fastest test is the origin.

147
00:10:29,065 --> 00:10:32,310
g of zero is minus three, not zero.

148
00:10:32,749 --> 00:10:34,504
So g is not linear.

149
00:10:34,929 --> 00:10:40,102
It is affine: the linear map five x, followed by a shift of minus three.

150
00:10:40,528 --> 00:10:44,890
Its graph is a straight line, but the line does not pass through the origin.

151
00:10:46,606 --> 00:10:47,936
Let's check it in NumPy.

152
00:10:48,348 --> 00:10:55,888
We build the matrix from where e one and e two go, apply it, and test the linear rule with two vectors.

153
00:10:56,313 --> 00:11:04,279
We test the shift and the square, compose the shear and the projection, and collapse two toy layers into one matrix.

154
00:11:05,806 --> 00:11:09,835
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

155
00:11:10,247 --> 00:11:12,574
A times three, two is four, five.

156
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,005
Both sides of the rule give twenty-one, twelve.

157
00:11:16,444 --> 00:11:21,191
The shift gives six versus seven, and the square gives four versus two.

158
00:11:21,617 --> 00:11:26,511
The two orders of composition agree, and the two layers equal one matrix.

159
00:11:28,239 --> 00:11:28,904
In summary.

160
00:11:29,343 --> 00:11:37,109
A linear mapping respects adding and scaling: f of a u plus b v equals a f of u plus b f of v.

161
00:11:37,548 --> 00:11:43,838
It keeps grid lines straight, parallel and evenly spaced, and it keeps the origin fixed.

162
00:11:44,276 --> 00:11:48,053
A shift is affine, and a square is not linear at all.

163
00:11:48,959 --> 00:11:54,119
Every linear map is a matrix whose columns are the images of the basis vectors.

164
00:11:54,531 --> 00:11:56,885
Composition is the matrix product.

165
00:11:57,337 --> 00:12:01,233
The kernel goes to zero, and the image is all outputs.

166
00:12:01,672 --> 00:12:07,111
Next, in video five: the inner product, one number that measures similarity.

167
00:12:07,536 --> 00:12:08,095
See you there.

168
00:12:10,006 --> 00:12:11,828
This video was made by David R.

169
00:12:12,267 --> 00:12:15,498
You can find more lessons at David Ramirez J dot com.

170
00:12:16,056 --> 00:12:16,774
Thanks for watching.
